I’ll start by reading the metadata, pattern layout, and the provided PXRD workflow guidance, then build a chemically constrained phase inventory before testing/refining candidates. I’ll keep the final artifacts tied to a replayed GSAS-II project.

The chemistry points first to Mn–W–O products (especially wolframite-type MnWO₄), but the task explicitly allows residual oxides and off-stoichiometric intermediates. I’m now extracting peak families and querying the frozen structure index so that phase inclusion is driven by unique reflections, not just the nominal reaction.

The dominant peak set matches monoclinic wolframite MnWO₄ (P2/c) across low, mid, and high angles. The only exact frozen entry is a 2.4 GPa single-crystal record, so I’m treating it as a starting topology—not as proof of the sample state—and will compare it with ambient-pressure wolframite analogues after the nuisance/profile baseline is established.

The first whole-pattern refinement confirms the MnWO₄ cell and a plausible specimen displacement (about −0.44 mm), but it also exposes an unacceptable profile surrogate: unconstrained U went strongly negative. I’m rejecting that profile, resetting to a nonnegative common baseline, and testing crystallite-size/strain broadening separately before judging minor phases or texture.

The width trials are replay-stable: isotropic size broadening (~0.10–0.14 μm) is supported, while strain-only broadening is substantially worse. Instrumental W and crystallite size remain partly correlated for this effectively single-phase pattern; I’m retaining a bounded common profile (U=0, W=40, X=1) plus the refined size and now testing the obvious coherent low-angle intensity deficit as surface roughness, with polarization as a separate control.

The source challenge is decisive: the exact MnWO₄ record gives the best fit; ambient Fe/Zn/Mg wolframite substitutions are worse under the same background, geometry, cell, size, and roughness model (Rwp ≈5.58–5.96% versus 5.45–5.48%; one DFT-like Fe model is much worse). I’m retaining the exact topology, while documenting its high-pressure provenance and the refined ambient-like cell. Next I’m re-challenging residual Mn/O and W/O phases against this cleaned profile.

A repeated low-angle asymmetry is real: the initial SH/L=0.02 baseline was inadequate. A bounded grid reveals a much better basin near SH/L≈0.08 (Rwp falls from 5.42% to 4.70%) without adding a phase. I’m resolving that basin carefully because SH/L, displacement, and size are correlated; the earlier weak WO₃ scales must be retested after this profile correction.

The common profile now behaves physically: positive U/W and the high-SH/L basin fit isolated peaks much better. Size and X are exactly tradeable for this one phase, while adding isotropic strain helps only when U is lower; that is the expected identifiability limit rather than evidence for a second phase. I’m testing the three symmetry-distinct uniaxial strain directions before freezing the simplest replay-stable width model.

Texture is unsupported: all five March–Dollase axes refine to 0.997–1.008, so I’ve pruned it. A small polarization correction is plausible (≈0.755) but has only marginal profile leverage. With the common peak shape now clean, I’m re-running the WO₃/Mn-oxide watch list; any phase that was merely absorbing the old asymmetry should collapse.

The cleaned-profile re-challenge leaves MnWO₄ as the only defensible crystalline phase. Alternate WO₃ and manganese-oxide candidates either collapse to zero or give unstable sub-percent scales without coherent unique reflections. I’m now freezing the supported profile model, replaying the project from disk, and generating the CIF/profile/JSON/report from that exact saved state.

The retained broadening terms are physically supported: removing crystallite size or microstrain worsens the fit, and the [001] uniaxial strain model outperforms the other symmetry-distinct axes. Preferred-orientation trials all refined back to essentially unity, so texture is being removed. I’m freezing the correlated but physically plausible source/profile estimates before the final replay so they cannot act as scale surrogates.

The saved-project forward replay now passes cleanly: it finds the original 8,301-point scan, one atomic MnWO₄ phase at normalized fraction 1.000, regenerates the profile without optimization, and raises no negative-width, background, displacement, or polarization flags. Replay Rwp is 4.1197%, matching the exported curve. I’m doing the final CIF geometry and file-consistency checks, then writing the concise evidence/uncertainty report.

Completed. The crystalline phase is wolframite MnWO₄, P2/c, at 100 wt% normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Final Rwp = 4.1197% and GOF = 1.1923.

Sub-percent oxide impurities below roughly 0.5 wt% and amorphous material cannot be reliably quantified or excluded.

